[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Entity Graphs in Hibernate: FetchGraph vs LoadGraph"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When working with ORM frameworks like Hibernate, the N+1 select problem and unintended lazy loading exceptions are common pitfalls. Hibernate Entity Graphs provide a flexible, declarative way to define which associations should be fetched alongside the primary entity. Understanding the distinct behaviors of FetchGraph and LoadGraph is crucial for optimizing database performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is an Entity Graph?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An Entity Graph is a blueprint that defines a subgraph of associations to be loaded from the database in a single query. Instead of changing entity fetching strategies globally via FetchType.EAGER, Entity Graphs allow you to customize fetching behavior on a per-query basis."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining an Entity Graph"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define an entity graph using the @NamedEntityGraph annotation directly on your entity class. Below is an example of an Order entity with a graph that includes its associated Customer and OrderItems."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@NamedEntityGraph(
    name = "Order.full",
    attributeNodes = {
        @NamedAttributeNode("customer"),
        @NamedAttributeNode("orderItems")
    }
)
public class Order {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private Customer customer;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<OrderItem> orderItems = new ArrayList<>();
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"FetchGraph vs LoadGraph: The Core Difference"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary difference between FetchGraph and LoadGraph lies in how they treat attributes that are NOT explicitly mentioned in the graph. Both graphs will eagerly fetch the attributes specified in the graph definition, but their handling of default fetching rules diverges significantly."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"FetchGraph: Acts as a strict whitelist. Attributes specified in the graph are fetched eagerly. All other attributes revert to LAZY fetching, regardless of their default annotations (@Basic(fetch=EAGER) or @ManyToOne(fetch=EAGER))."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LoadGraph: Acts as an additive overlay. Attributes specified in the graph are fetched eagerly. However, any other attributes defined as EAGER by default on the entity will ALSO be fetched normally."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Using FetchGraph in JPA / Hibernate"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To apply a FetchGraph in your query, use the hint name "javax.persistence.fetchgraph" (or "jakarta.persistence.fetchgraph" in newer EE versions). This ensures only the mapped fields are loaded."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"EntityGraph<?> graph = entityManager.getEntityGraph("Order.full");

Map<String, Object> hints = new HashMap<>();
hints.put("jakarta.persistence.fetchgraph", graph);

Order order = entityManager.find(Order.class, 1L, hints);"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Using LoadGraph in JPA / Hibernate"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To apply a LoadGraph, use the hint name "jakarta.persistence.loadgraph". This guarantees that your explicitly requested graph attributes are loaded, while preserving any other default eager relationships."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"EntityGraph<?> graph = entityManager.getEntityGraph("Order.full");

Map<String, Object> hints = new HashMap<>();
hints.put("jakarta.persistence.loadgraph", graph);

Order order = entityManager.find(Order.class, 1L, hints);"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Summary"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer FetchGraph when you want precise, strict control over the SQL payload to avoid over-fetching data."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use LoadGraph when you want to augment the default eager loading behavior without manually specifying every standard eager relation."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always keep entities configured with LAZY fetching by default, and leverage Entity Graphs to override fetching strategies dynamically at runtime."}]
